# Regression test: a job that's marked `Terminated` but never actually dies (e.g. an
# internal loop that doesn't check its own control channel), and is later `resume`d,
# must show up again as `Running` -- not stay hidden forever just because it was once
# `Terminated`.
#
# Root cause: `JobData::terminated_reported` (see `src/lang/state/global_state.rs`),
# added to guarantee a terminated job is reported by `jobs` at most once (see
# `tests/terminate_then_double_listing_shows_terminated_once.crush`), had no check on
# whether the job's *current* live status had moved away from `Terminated` -- only
# `snapshot` cleared it, and only to re-arm it for a fresh `terminate`, not to un-hide
# an already-flagged job. But status can leave `Terminated`: `resume`/
# `resume_foreground`/`pause` all set it back to `Running`/`Paused` regardless of its
# current value. Reproduced directly: `terminate` a backgrounded `while {$true} {}`
# loop (which ignores the signal entirely and keeps running), list `jobs` twice (both
# correctly showed it once, then hidden), then `resume` it -- before the fix, every
# listing afterward showed nothing for it at all, even though its thread was still
# genuinely running; after the fix, it shows up again with status `Running`.
#
# Fixed by only suppressing a job while it's observably *still* `Terminated` (either
# still alive with that same live status, or already fully gone) -- if its live status
# has since moved away from `Terminated`, it's reported again under its current
# status, and `terminated_reported` is cleared so a later, genuinely new termination
# gets its own one-time report too.
#
# `terminate` is called immediately after starting the loop, before anything else that
# could fail: a `while {$true} {}` loop ignores its own control channel entirely and
# never actually stops, and (confirmed directly) a script that ends with such a job
# still running *and never terminated* hangs waiting for it at exit -- `terminate`
# itself is enough to let the process exit cleanly despite the loop still spinning
# (crush's own exit path doesn't wait on a job once it's been told to stop, whether or
# not the job's own thread actually honors that), so issuing it first makes every
# later assertion below safe to fail without hanging the whole test run.
$job_id := $({while {$true} {}} &)
terminate $job_id

# First listing after terminate: reported once, Terminated.
$first_rows := $(jobs | where {($id == $job_id)} | materialize)
$first_count := $($first_rows | count)
assert ($first_count == 1) "a terminated job should be reported once on the first jobs listing after terminate"
$first_status := ($first_rows[0]):status
assert ($first_status == "Terminated") "the one-time report should have status Terminated"

# Second listing: already reported once, and still Terminated (the loop ignores the
# signal and keeps running) -- hidden.
$second_rows := $(jobs | where {($id == $job_id)} | materialize)
$second_count := $($second_rows | count)
assert ($second_count == 0) "a terminated job already reported once should be hidden on the next listing, as long as it's still Terminated"

resume $job_id

# After resume: no longer Terminated (the loop was never actually stopped) -- must
# show up again, as Running, not stay hidden.
$third_rows := $(jobs | where {($id == $job_id)} | materialize)
$third_count := $($third_rows | count)
assert ($third_count == 1) "a job resumed after being marked Terminated (but never actually stopped) must show up again"
$third_status := ($third_rows[0]):status
assert ($third_status == "Running") "a job resumed after being marked Terminated should show its current status, Running"

terminate $job_id
echo "done"
